From a3de7bb4fbf8c008d3606dd9dfe0731d5162ba83 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: adbtrr_work8_fin_tc <adbtrr_work8_fin_tc@local>
Date: Tue, 8 Sep 2026 06:41:13 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/9] os/kernel/paging: Add ASSERT hitting test case for
 pg_worker

pg_callback() hands the result of an asynchronous page fill to the page
fill worker thread in g_fillresult, with g_pftcb naming the task the fill
was for. A fill which completed with an error cannot be retried and the
task blocked on it can never run again, so pg_worker() refuses to
continue through ASSERT(g_fillresult == OK).

g_fillresult is file scope state of pg_worker.c, so add
pg_worker_asserttest() next to it, built only when the test case is
enabled. It presents a fill in progress which completed with -EIO and
wakes the worker, which is the state the assertion refuses to continue
from. The assertion is taken in the worker thread, so the kernel side
helper waits a few work periods for the worker to run.

ASSERT() carries no CONFIG_DEBUG dependency, so the test case works on a
build with the debug features turned off. The DEBUGASSERT() in pg_miss()
is not used here for that reason. The assertion only exists on the
asynchronous fill path, so CONFIG_PAGING_BLOCKINGFILL must be disabled.

 - os/kernel/paging/pg_worker.c : test only entry point for the fill
   completion path.
 - os/kernel/paging/paging.h : declaration of the entry point.
 - os/drivers/os_api_test/kernel/test_assert_paging.c : kernel side
   helper reached through the TESTIOC_ASSERT_PAGING ioctl.
 - apps/examples/testcase/le_tc/kernel/tc_assert_paging.c : user side
   test case. A return from the ioctl is a test case failure.

Controlled by CONFIG_TC_KERNEL_ASSERT_PAGING, default n, depends on
CONFIG_PAGING and on the asynchronous fill path. It crashes the board by
design and must be enabled alone.
